// The `units` namespace (area 19, "Physical quantities").
// Each operation is listed, typed, in `jadpo-std`'s operation table, which
// names the Rust function in `jadpo-value` that runs it; a test holds
// these stubs to that table.
//
// A quantity is held in its kind's SI base unit at the kind's scale, so a
// unit exists only where a value is made or shown. `Quantity` is any
// kind, fixed at each call by the value or by the unit given, and
// `QuantityUnit` a variant of one kind's unit enum (`LengthUnit.mile`).
// `+`, `-`, comparisons and the products and quotients of two kinds are
// the standard operator table's; these operations say what no operator
// says. `in` is a keyword, so its declaration takes a trailing `_`, and a
// call writes `units.in`.

public library units {
    /// `value` in `unit`, a unit of its kind, as a `Decimal` of `scale`
    /// places rounded by `rounding`: `units.in(5km, LengthUnit.mile, scale:
    /// 2, rounding: Rounding.half_even)` is `3.11`.
    function in_(value: Quantity, unit: QuantityUnit, scale: Int, rounding: Rounding) -> Decimal {}

    /// `value` of `unit`, an `Int` or a `Decimal`, as the quantity of
    /// `unit`'s kind, converted exactly to the base unit and rounded half to
    /// even to the kind's scale: `units.of(5, LengthUnit.mile)`.
    function of(value: Number, unit: QuantityUnit) -> Quantity {}

    /// The items added up, all of one kind; nothing for no items. A
    /// `Temperature` or a `SoundLevel` doesn't add to itself, so neither is
    /// summed.
    function sum(items: List<Quantity>) -> Quantity {}
}
